From b75d1f581794f85c6e594941ed7d4e29e686c568 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Theorem Labs
Date: Mon, 17 Aug 2026 22:37:23 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] [sanitizer_common] cover musl's thread descriptor in the TLS
 range
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

🤖: On musl ThreadDescriptorSize() returns 0 and GetTls() hands lsan a range
that stops at the thread pointer, so the thread descriptor -- which holds
the libc state a thread carries, including the pointer to its
thread-specific data -- is never scanned as a root, and an allocation
referenced only from there is reported as a leak.

musl now exports sizeof(struct pthread) as __pthread_struct_size, since
the size is neither part of its ABI nor computable from outside and
changes with the architecture and the version.  Read it through a weak
reference, so a libc without the symbol still links and keeps the old
behaviour, and extend the TLS range by it.

The two halves of this reach different architectures.  Making
ThreadDescriptorSize() answer for musl is arch-neutral, and on x86_64 it
is the whole change: GetTls() there takes the branch written for a TLS
variant II target, which sets the range to end at the thread pointer plus
ThreadDescriptorSize(), and that covers a descriptor sitting at the thread
pointer as soon as the size stops being zero.  The second hunk, which
moves the start of the range down, is in the branch a variant I target
takes and is dead code on x86_64.

Only that variant I path is exercised here -- measured on aarch64 musl
1.2.6, the descriptor is 200 bytes and the scanned range now starts 16
bytes into it, the gap that variant leaves above the thread pointer.  The
two words that fall outside are the self pointer and one thread list link.
So the two architectures do not end up equally covered: on x86_64 the
descriptor is scanned whole, on aarch64 all but its first 16 bytes.

Both of those extend whatever GetStaticTlsBoundary returned, and it
returns an empty range when no module has a PT_TLS -- the case its own
comment says can happen on musl.  Extending an empty range by a descriptor
size that is no longer zero produces a range that is not there: on a
variant I target *addr wraps past zero, on x86_64 the range starts at
address 0.  So return early on an empty range instead.  Nothing reaches
that branch under ASan, whose per-thread fake stack pointer puts a PT_TLS
in every binary it links, but the failure only became possible with this
change, so the guard belongs with it.

This does not make pthread_setspecific values reachable the way it does on
glibc, where they live inside the descriptor.  On musl the main thread's
are in a global array, which lsan already scans, and a spawned thread's
sit above its static TLS blocks, which nothing covers; that would need
musl to export the range of the block as well.
---
 .../sanitizer_linux_libcdep.cpp               | 34 +++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 34 insertions(+)

diff --git a/compiler-rt/lib/sanitizer_common/sanitizer_linux_libcdep.cpp b/compiler-rt/lib/sanitizer_common/sanitizer_linux_libcdep.cpp
index 331e1c7d8..7ac279029 100644
--- a/compiler-rt/lib/sanitizer_common/sanitizer_linux_libcdep.cpp
+++ b/compiler-rt/lib/sanitizer_common/sanitizer_linux_libcdep.cpp
@@ -391,6 +391,23 @@ static uptr TlsPreTcbSize() {
   return kTlsPreTcbSize;
 }
 #    endif
+#  elif SANITIZER_LINUX && !SANITIZER_ANDROID && !SANITIZER_GO
+// musl exports the size of its thread descriptor (sizeof(struct pthread)),
+// which is neither part of its ABI nor computable from outside.  GetTls() uses
+// it to bring the descriptor into the range lsan scans as a root, so that an
+// allocation whose only reference is in the libc state a thread carries there
+// is not reported as a leak.  Unlike glibc, musl does not keep the values set
+// with pthread_setspecific inside the descriptor -- the main thread's are in a
+// global array, and a spawned thread's sit above its static TLS blocks -- so
+// this does not cover those.  The reference is weak because a libc without the
+// symbol must still link; then this returns 0 and nothing is covered, which is
+// the behaviour that was here before.
+extern "C" __attribute__((weak)) const usize __pthread_struct_size;
+
+void InitTlsSize() {}
+uptr ThreadDescriptorSize() {
+  return &__pthread_struct_size ? __pthread_struct_size : 0;
+}
 #  else   // (SANITIZER_FREEBSD || SANITIZER_GLIBC) && !SANITIZER_GO
 void InitTlsSize() {}
 uptr ThreadDescriptorSize() { return 0; }
@@ -592,6 +609,12 @@ static void GetTls(uptr *addr, uptr *size) {
 #    elif SANITIZER_FREEBSD || SANITIZER_LINUX || SANITIZER_SOLARIS
   uptr align;
   GetStaticTlsBoundary(addr, size, &align);
+  // No module has a PT_TLS, which its own comment says can happen on musl: the
+  // range is empty and there is nothing below to extend.  Extending it anyway
+  // wraps, on a variant I target by subtracting the thread descriptor size from
+  // zero, so stop here and leave it empty.
+  if (*size == 0)
+    return;
 #      if defined(__x86_64__) || defined(__i386__) || defined(__s390__) || \
           defined(__sparc__)
   if (SANITIZER_GLIBC) {
@@ -635,6 +658,17 @@ static void GetTls(uptr *addr, uptr *size) {
   *addr -= tcb_size;
   *size += tcb_size;
 #          endif
+#        elif SANITIZER_LINUX && !SANITIZER_ANDROID
+  // musl. The thread descriptor sits directly below the thread pointer, so
+  // extending the range down by its size covers it. On a TLS variant I target
+  // the static TLS blocks start a little above the thread pointer, and the
+  // range is shifted up by that gap, so this can miss the first few words of
+  // the descriptor -- self and the thread list links, which hold nothing lsan
+  // is looking for. ThreadDescriptorSize() is 0 on a libc that does not export
+  // its size, and then this changes nothing.
+  const uptr tcb_size = ThreadDescriptorSize();
+  *addr -= tcb_size;
+  *size += tcb_size;
 #        endif
 #      endif
 #    elif SANITIZER_NETBSD
